Yes, I realize they are not (exactly) the same thing, but "orthography," as
commonly understood, is not devoid of typographic conventions; those are
_part_ of an orthography, at least as linguists use it--when one says that
they developed an orthography for a previously-unwritten language, no one
would assume that they had figured out how to spell things, but not bothered
with how to actually write things down.

And I did check the definitions, from several places, and I see nothing that
contradicts anything I said, nor anything anyone else said.  I meant what I
said before, that the line is, apparently, drawn at different places for
others than it is for me.

